= numpy

This chapter teaches numpy before anything downstream uses it. The version is
the first fact: numpy 2.5.3, pinned in `books/python/requirements.txt`,
installed into the gate's venv, and asserted from inside the first sample,
because search engines still surface numpy 2.x answers by volume and the
behavior this chapter pins changed across that line. #xref-to("python",
"capstone2") builds its analytics leg on the arrays introduced here, and
#xref-to("python", "pandas") builds its frames on the same arrays next
chapter. The float semantics underneath are the ones
#xref-to("python", "numerics") already pinned: binary64, the neighbor
double, and `math.isclose`. The stdlib chapter handles scalars one at a
time; numpy handles a million of them as one buffer, and every claim below
is an ok line in the four samples, 38 in total, or a sentence quoted from
the numpy 2.5 manual fetched 2026-09-12.

== the ndarray

An ndarray is one block of memory plus the metadata that walks it. The
manual defines the walking triple in three lines: `ndarray.shape`, "Tuple
of array dimensions", `ndarray.strides`, "Tuple of bytes to step in each
dimension when traversing an array", and `ndarray.itemsize`, "Length of
one array element in bytes". A 3 by 4 grid of int32 pins all three at
once, strides `(16, 4)`, four bytes per element and sixteen to jump a row,
and `asfortranarray` walks the same numbers the other way, `(4, 12)`:

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/ndarray.py", first: 68, last: 74, caption: [the chapter contract: the interpreter pin, then the numpy 2.5.3 pin asserted in-sample])

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/ndarray.py", first: 8, last: 18, caption: [shape, dtype, strides, itemsize, nbytes, size, ndim pinned as one tuple])

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/ndarray.py", first: 21, last: 31, caption: [fortran order flips the strides, and the four everyday constructors with the dtype each was asked for])

The constructors are chosen by shape: `zeros` and `ones` take a shape,
`arange` takes a step like `range`, and `linspace` takes a count and
includes both endpoints, 0.0 through 1.0 in five values. The deeper half
of the section is the view and copy split, and the manual states it as a
rule, not a tendency: "All arrays generated by basic slicing are always
views of the original array", while "Advanced indexing always returns a
copy of the data (contrast with basic slicing that returns a view)". The
sample asserts both halves on one owner:

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/ndarray.py", first: 34, last: 55, caption: [five index expressions that stay views, five that own their memory, and shares_memory as the judge])

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/ndarray.py", first: 58, last: 65, caption: [a view write lands in the owner, a copy write never does])

The `base` attribute answers who owns the buffer, and the sample uses a
literal `np.array` as its owner on purpose: `base` reports the memory
owner, not the last expression. Slicing an array that is itself a reshape
reports the original buffer, not the intermediate, so `base` is for
ownership questions and `np.shares_memory` for relation questions.

