== vectorization and broadcasting

The before and after is one loop. The manual sells broadcasting as exactly
that: "Broadcasting provides a means of vectorizing array operations so
that looping occurs in C instead of Python." The sample runs both forms on
the same four temperatures and asserts they agree to the last bit, the
python loop appending one division at a time, the ufunc expression making
one pass over the buffer:

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/broadcast.py", first: 6, last: 11, caption: [the loop and the ufunc expression, asserted equal element for element])

The shape rules are three sentences. "When operating on two arrays, NumPy
compares their shapes element-wise. It starts with the trailing (i.e.
rightmost) dimension and works its way left." Then the compatibility test:
"Two dimensions are compatible when 1. they are equal, or 2. one of them
is 1", and when neither holds, "a `ValueError: operands could not be
broadcast together` exception is thrown, indicating that the arrays have
incompatible shapes". The failing message quotes both shapes verbatim on
this machine, `(2,3)` against `(4,)`:

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/broadcast.py", first: 14, last: 28, caption: [a (4, 1) column plus a (3,) row stretches into the full (4, 3) grid])

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/broadcast.py", first: 31, last: 39, caption: [the mismatch quotes its shapes in the error text])

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/broadcast.py", first: 42, last: 51, caption: [broadcast_to stretches without copying, and the result is read-only])

Reductions give the axis its meaning. `sum(axis=0)` folds down the
columns, `sum(axis=1)` folds along the rows, `axis=None` flattens first,
negative indices count from the last axis, and `keepdims` keeps the folded
axis at size 1 so a downstream broadcast still lines up. The dtype rules
come along for free and are worth pinning: an int32 array sums to int64
and means to float64, a float32 array stays float32 through its sum:

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/broadcast.py", first: 105, last: 117, caption: [axis 0, axis 1, axis None, axis -1, and keepdims, each with its result pinned])

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/broadcast.py", first: 54, last: 60, caption: [int32 sums to int64 and means to float64, float32 keeps its dtype])

The empty case splits by identity. An empty float array sums to 0.0, the
identity for addition, and refuses a max with "zero-size array to
reduction operation maximum which has no identity":

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/broadcast.py", first: 63, last: 70, caption: [empty sums to the identity, empty max refuses with the identity message])

