== dtypes and promotion

Promotion is the numpy 2 line in the sand, specified by NEP 50 and
enforced since 2.0: "We propose to remove all value-based logic and add
special handling for Python scalars to preserve some convenient
behaviors. Python scalars will be considered 'weakly' typed." The
principle behind it, "Values must never influence result type", and the
operating rule, "At no time is the value used to decide the result of
this promotion. The value is only considered when it is converted to the
new dtype; this may raise an error", are both quotable from the nep. In
practice: a python int or float adopts the array's dtype, so an int32
array plus `1` stays int32 and a float32 array plus `1.5` stays float32,
while a kind change goes to the minimum float, float64. A numpy scalar or
a second array is strong: int32 meets int64 and int64 wins, uint8 meets
int8 and the signed result is int16, uint64 meets int64 and the result
is float64, because no integer dtype holds both:

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/dtypes.py", first: 6, last: 12, caption: [python scalars are weak, the array dtype survives, kind changes go float64])

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/dtypes.py", first: 15, last: 21, caption: [numpy scalars are strong, and uint64 with int64 lands in float64])

The error side is the point of the design, errors over silent casting.
An int8 array plus `256` raises `OverflowError: Python integer 256 out
of bounds for int8`. A uint64 array plus `-1` refuses the same way.
Writing `2**40` into an int32 slot raises "Python int too large to
convert to C long", the windows flavor of the boundary, where a C long
is four bytes. In place operations keep their dtype when they can, a
float32 array absorbs `+= 1.5`, and refuse with the same_kind casting
message when they cannot, an int32 array plus `1.5` in place:

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/dtypes.py", first: 24, last: 39, caption: [three overflow refusals with the exact message text, never a silent wrap])

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/dtypes.py", first: 42, last: 50, caption: [in place keeps float32, int32 in place refuses float under same_kind])

One silent case survives, and it is worth knowing which one. A weak
python scalar that fits the dtype is converted with no complaint, so a
uint8 array holding 250 plus `10` wraps to 4 modulo 256, only the
scalar-on-scalar form warns. The value is bounded by the dtype, not by
the operation, which is the NEP 50 bargain: predictable types, and the
programmer carries the range check.

Strings come in two dtypes. The historical `<U5` fixes the width at
array creation, wasted bytes for short entries, truncation beyond it.
`np.dtypes.StringDType()` is the variable width dtype with a real `None`
for missing entries, and the `np.strings` module carries the ufuncs,
`upper` on either dtype. Adding an int to either is a TypeError, "ufunc
'add' did not contain a loop with signature matching types", because
there is no cast to fall back on:

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/dtypes.py", first: 53, last: 70, caption: [fixed U widths, StringDType and its None, np.strings on both, and the no-loop TypeError])

Dates are integer counts with units. The manual: "The date units are
years ('Y'), months ('M'), weeks ('W'), and days ('D'), while the time
units are hours ('h'), minutes ('m'), seconds ('s'), milliseconds
('ms'), and some additional SI-prefix seconds-based units", and for
calendars, "NumPy includes a set of 'busday' (business day) functions".
A week of days builds with `arange`, subtraction yields a timedelta64,
adding a one day timedelta shifts the week, 90 minutes cast to hours
truncates to 1, and monday through friday of the book's reference week
counts 5:

#listing("python/samples/src/Ch24/dtypes.py", first: 73, last: 83, caption: [datetime64 units, timedelta arithmetic, and the business day count])

The pandas handoff closes the chapter. #xref-to("python", "pandas")
stores its text in the str dtype and its numbers in these dtypes, and
`to_numpy` hands the buffer over, which is why the two libraries compose
without copying in the common path.

